    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Background:\r\nDespite calls for increased human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) testing, emergency departments have lower testing rates compared to other healthcare settings. This is, in part, given that many emergency department physicians cite worry that implementing preventative services like HIV testing would increase emergency department visit length of stay. However, it is unclear what impact HIV testing has on visit length of stay.\r\nObjective:\r\nThe goal of this work was to use a nationally representative sample of emergency department visits to estimate the impact of HIV testing on emergency department visit length of stay.\r\nMethods:\r\nWe completed a cross-sectional analysis of the 2018-2021 National Hospital Ambulatory Care Survey (NHAMCS), which provides weighted estimates of ambulatory care services provided in United States emergency departments. Initial analyses were conducted on the pooled dataset of all four years and sensitivity analyses of 2018-2019 and 2020-2021 grouped data were conducted to investigate possible discrepancies due to the COVID-19 pandemic. Negative binomial regression models, both univariate and multivariable, were used to explore the relationship between our primary predictor (whether a HIV test was performed) and our primary outcome of interest (length of visit stay in minutes).\r\nResults:\r\nOverall median length of stay for visits that included an HIV test was longer (254 [interquartile range (IQR): 142-425] minutes) than visits that did not include an HIV test (177 [107-280] minutes). A difference in length of stay persisted when looking at year groupings (2018-2019, 2020-2021), but results were mixed when stratifying by triage immediacy. In regression, HIV testing was associated with a higher unadjusted (RR = 1.44 [95% confidence interval (CI): 1.24- 1.68]) and adjusted (aRR = 1.23 [1.10-1.39]) rate of increased length of stay; this finding persisted when looking at the 2018-2019 visits, but the association was not significant for 2020- 2021 visits.\r\nConclusion:\r\nIn this nationally representative sampling of emergency department visits from 2018-2021, visits with an HIV test had a longer median stay than those without. This difference persisted for all year groupings but not for all levels of triage immediacy. After accounting for possible confounders, HIV testing was also associated with longer patient length of stay overall, but further work is needed to better understand potential differences due to the COVID-19 pandemic.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
